# Master plan: MQC security hardening (issues #1–#12 + #6a)

## Status

- Consolidates all 13 issue plans in
  `socket-level-wrapper-MQC/mqc-issue-*.plan` into a single
  ship order.
- This file is the **orchestration** document.  Per-issue plans
  remain authoritative for what each change does and how each
  test runs; this file is "in what order, in what cutover, with
  what dependency."

## End-state vision

After all phases land, MQC v0 has:

- Full handshake-transcript binding (every byte, every signature,
  every derived key).
- Mandatory mutual revocation enforcement, fail-closed by default.
- Cert objects validated as self-verifying (cosignature, inclusion
  proof, validity window, cosigner-fingerprint pinning).
- Client-side expected-identity enforcement (TLS-style hostname
  binding to the dialed name).
- Strict JSON parsing on every handshake field with cheap
  pre-crypto length filtering.
- Layered DoS defenses (per-IP, per-cert, per-handshake-bytes,
  bounded cert cache).
- Operationally tunable knobs in `/etc/postWolf/config` for every
  parameter that should be reachable without recompile.

The protocol stays at draft-page-mqc-protocol-**00**; spec edits
land within that draft.  Per the deployment-model note at the top
of [`README-plans.md`](./README-plans.md), no backwards-compat
shims are needed — server and clients always upgrade together.

## Execution mode (read this before starting)

When a Claude Code session is opened to execute this master plan,
the operator (the user) will start it in a high-permission mode
(`bypassPermissions` or equivalent).  Within that session, Claude
should take **full latitude on every action** — file edits, bash
commands, builds, test runs, service restarts on factsorlie.com,
config edits, redeploys — without pausing to confirm each step.

The **single exception** is `git commit` (and by extension
`git push`).  Per `CLAUDE.md` and the existing
`feedback_commit_granularity.md` memory entry, commit boundaries
remain explicitly user-controlled: at each commit boundary in the
"Suggested commit boundaries" table below, Claude **stops and
asks** before running `git commit`.  The user reviews the staged
diff, confirms the commit message and granularity, and then
authorizes the commit.

This applies for the duration of the plan's execution.  After the
plan is fully landed (Phase 6 exit criteria met), this mode lapses
and normal interactive defaults resume.

Rationale: the plan is large (10 commits, ~5 KLoC of code + tests
+ docs across 6 phases), and pausing for permission on every
file edit would multiply the wall-clock cost without gaining
review fidelity (the user reviews at commit boundaries, not
at every Edit call).  Commits remain gated because that's the
durable artifact in the repo and the unit the user wants
explicit control over.

## Phase ordering (TL;DR)

```
P0 ─► P1 (flag day) ─► P2 ─► P3 ─► P4 ─► P5 ─► P6 ─► P7
6a    1,2,3,4,5         7,8,9 11,12 system spec   user-  encrypted-
      6+10 close                    testing cleanup facing  identity
      automatically                                docs    rewrite
```

- Phases 0–3 are **implementation** (per-issue plans, per-issue code).
- Phase 4 is **integration testing** (cross-issue, system-level).
- Phase 5 is **spec consolidation** (technical reference for
  implementers).
- Phase 6 is **user-facing documentation** (operators, CLI users,
  future Claude Code sessions).
- Phase 7 is the **encrypted-identity mode rewrite** (deferred —
  pre-Phase-1 implementation was deleted; clear mode is the
  default; encrypted mode is opt-in for callers that need
  traffic-analysis resistance).

## Phase 0 — Operability foundation (ship-anytime)

**Plan**: [`mqc-issue-6a.plan`](./mqc-issue-6a.plan)

**What changes**: nothing on the wire.  Adds a runtime-config
loader (`read_config_long` next to the existing
`read_config_url`), promotes ten operational constants
(handshake timeouts, frame-size ceilings, rate-limit buckets,
cache TTLs, signature freshness window) to optional `[global]`
keys in `/etc/postWolf/config`.

**Why first**: zero-coordination, behavior-conservative
(absent keys = today's behavior), and provides the config
infrastructure that Phase 2's `mqc-revocation-policy` plugs into.

**Cutover**: none.  Build, install, restart, optionally edit
`/etc/postWolf/config`.  No client coordination.

**Exit criteria**:
- Build is warning-clean.
- `make check` is green.
- `journalctl` shows no per-key config lines if no keys were set
  (i.e., default behavior preserved exactly).
- Setting one knob (e.g., `mqc-handshake-stall-sec 1`) is
  observable both in `journalctl` startup output and in actual
  behavior change.

---

## Phase 1 — Handshake hardening (one flag-day cutover)

**Plans (bundled into one commit and one cutover)**:

| Plan | What it adds | Wire impact |
|---|---|---|
| [`mqc-issue-1.plan`](./mqc-issue-1.plan) | Full transcript binding; `version`, `suite`, `mode` fields; `kem_pub` field rename; ML-DSA `ctx` label | Wire-format break (new fields, renamed fields, new signature semantics) |
| [`mqc-issue-2.plan`](./mqc-issue-2.plan) | HKDF info strings tag version + suite | Wire-quiet, behavior-breaking |
| [`mqc-issue-3.plan`](./mqc-issue-3.plan) | `HKDF-Extract(transcript_hash, SS)` + per-direction IVs | Wire-quiet, behavior-breaking |
| [`mqc-issue-4.plan`](./mqc-issue-4.plan) | Finished MAC frame each direction post-handshake | Wire-impact (new frames after handshake) |
| [`mqc-issue-5.plan`](./mqc-issue-5.plan) | 31-byte AAD on every AEAD call (label, version, direction, frame_type, seq, length) | Wire-quiet, behavior-breaking |

**Auto-close** (no separate work):

- [`mqc-issue-6.plan`](./mqc-issue-6.plan) — encrypted-identity auth
  gaps; closed by issue-1's transcript construction including
  EK_c, CT_s, mode, role, both `cert_index` values, version, suite.
- [`mqc-issue-10.plan`](./mqc-issue-10.plan) — mode downgrade;
  closed by issue-1's explicit `mode` field bound into the
  transcript.

**Why bundled**: every plan in this phase is wire-breaking or
behavior-breaking, every plan touches the same `mqc.c` handshake
functions, and the dependency chain (#1 ⇒ #3 ⇒ #4) has no
intermediate state worth being in production for.  Splitting into
multiple flag days serves no one and just multiplies cutover
windows.

**Why issue-2 is in the bundle even though it's largely subsumed
by #3**: the info-string tagging is two `#define` edits;
shipping it separately is more overhead than just landing it
with #1.  Once #3 lands, the version+suite tag in the info
string is no longer load-bearing for security but stays for
debugging readability.

**Cutover sequence**:

1. Single combined commit on `phase-20`.
2. Build + install on the dev box; verify all positive tests
   from each plan pass.  Verify all negative tests reject.
   Verify zero compiler warnings.
3. SSH to factsorlie.com:
   - `git pull`.
   - `./make-all.sh`.
   - `sudo systemctl restart mtc-ca.service`.
4. Redeploy the four MTC client tools (`show-tpm`, `bootstrap_ca`,
   `bootstrap_leaf`, `admin_recosign`) by relink — they all link
   `libmqc.a` and pick up the new behavior automatically.
5. Smoke test: `echo hi | mqc --encode --env --no-cache | mqc
   --decode --env --no-cache` → `hi`.
6. End-to-end: `show-tpm --verify` against factsorlie.com:8446
   succeeds.

**Window**: A few minutes.  In-flight TCP connections that span
the cutover fail at the next handshake; jobs are short and
re-runnable.

**Exit criteria**:
- All zero-warning build / `make check` / per-plan test gates pass.
- 24-hour soak after restart shows no MQC handshake or AEAD
  failures in `journalctl` for legitimate clients.
- Sequence-number contract is unified: data plane begins at
  `seq=1` in both modes (Finished consumed `seq=0`).

---

## Phase 2 — Verification-policy hardening (incremental, after Phase 1)

These three plans are independent of each other and can ship in
any order.  Each is a separate commit and a separate (small)
restart.  All three depend on Phase 1 having landed because
their security properties are meaningful only when the handshake
identity itself is unsubstitutable.  Plan #7 additionally depends
on Phase 0's config-file infrastructure.

### Phase 2a — [`mqc-issue-7.plan`](./mqc-issue-7.plan): mandatory revocation

**What changes**: revocation queries become mandatory by default
on **both** sides of the handshake (today only the server
checks); fail-closed on log-query failure.  New runtime knob
`mqc-revocation-policy` with values `mandatory` (default),
`cache-only`, `disabled`.

**Cutover**: rebuild + restart `mtc-ca.service`; redeploy client
tools.  No wire change.

### Phase 2b — [`mqc-issue-8.plan`](./mqc-issue-8.plan): cert self-verification audit + tightening

**What changes**: spec §10.1 rewrite making "untrusted transport"
explicit; new §10.6 cert validity window check
(`not_before`/`not_after` with ±300 s skew); cosigner-fingerprint
file alongside each cached cert so cosigner rotation invalidates
stale verified entries; 8 negative tests proving each §10 check
actually triggers.

**Cutover**: rebuild + restart; redeploy client tools.  No wire
change.

### Phase 2c — [`mqc-issue-9.plan`](./mqc-issue-9.plan): server-name / expected-identity check

**What changes**: client-side `mqc_cert_name_matches` check
gating the handshake on "the cert at this `cert_index` lists the
hostname I dialed."  New CLI flags `--expected-name` and
`--no-name-check`; fail-closed for IP literals.  Spec §10.7 +
§12.9.

**Pre-deploy audit**: confirm every deployed peer's cert lists
its public hostname (`show-tpm --list-peers`).  For
factsorlie.com, the audit before cutover.

**Cutover**: rebuild + restart; redeploy client tools.  No wire
change.

### Phase 2 exit criteria

- All three sub-phases land without regressions.
- 24-hour soak after each shows zero false-positive aborts on
  legitimate traffic.
- `mqc-revocation-policy` in operator's `/etc/postWolf/config`
  takes effect (verifiable via `journalctl`).

---

## Phase 3 — Input hardening + DoS (incremental, can ship-anytime)

These two plans share helpers and ship in order: #11 first
(introduces the strict-parser helpers), then #12 (consumes them).

### Phase 3a — [`mqc-issue-11.plan`](./mqc-issue-11.plan): strict JSON parsing

**What changes**: every handshake JSON parse uses
`JSON_TOKENER_STRICT` + `JSON_TOKENER_VALIDATE_UTF8`; trailing
garbage rejected; duplicate keys rejected; unknown top-level
fields rejected; per-field strict readers
(`mqc_json_get_int_strict`, `mqc_json_get_hex_strict`,
`mqc_json_get_string_strict`) replace bare `json_object_get_*`
calls.  Spec §5.2 strict-parsing paragraph; new §12.10.

**Cutover**: rebuild + restart; redeploy client tools.  No wire
change.  Wire-quiet AND behavior-conservative for legitimate
peers (postWolf controls all clients; their JSON is conformant).

### Phase 3b — [`mqc-issue-12.plan`](./mqc-issue-12.plan): cheap pre-crypto filters + DoS budgets

**What changes**: pre-crypto byte-length validation on `kem_pub`
and `signature` (rejects garbage before the ~400 µs ML-DSA
verify), per-(IP, cert_index) throttling, total
handshake-bytes-in-flight cap with atomic counter, bounded LRU
on cert-verify cache.  Four new runtime knobs:
`mqc-rl-cert-per-min`, `mqc-rl-cert-per-hour`,
`mqc-handshake-bytes-total-cap`, `mqc-cert-cache-max-entries`.
Spec §11.2 extension; §12.6 strengthening.

**Depends on**: 3a's `mqc_json_get_hex_strict(…, expected_byte_len,
…)` helper.

**Cutover**: rebuild + restart; redeploy client tools.

### Phase 3 exit criteria

- Strict-parsing rejections trigger only on attacker-shaped or
  malformed input, never on legitimate clients.
- Pre-crypto length checks block malformed handshakes before any
  ML-DSA call (verify with the per-test counter probe in 12).
- 24-hour soak shows clean logs.

---

## Phase 4 — System-level testing (after all code lands)

Each per-issue plan ships its own positive/negative unit-and-
harness tests; those are the load-bearing per-feature regression
nets.  Phase 4 is the **system-level** layer above that: the
suite-of-suites pass that proves the 13 plans compose correctly,
not just that each works in isolation.

### 4a — Aggregate regression suite

Bundle the per-plan tests into one harness target:

```
make -f Makefile.tools test-mqc-all
```

Runs every test from issues #1, #3, #4, #5, #6a, #7, #8, #9, #11,
#12 in one pass.  Total ~75 tests.  Must complete in under 10
minutes on a single dev box and exit zero.

### 4b — Cross-tool round-trip matrix

Every MQC client tool against the server, in every mode (clear /
encrypted), with every revocation policy (mandatory / cache-only /
disabled), with valid + expected-name-mismatch + revoked + expired
peer fixtures.  Matrix entries:

```
clients × {clear, enc} × {mandatory, cache-only, disabled}
        × {valid, mismatch, revoked, expired}
```

= 4 tools × 2 × 3 × 4 = **96 cells**.  Driver script enumerates
each, captures the expected pass/fail, asserts the actual outcome.
A regression in any cell = abort.

### 4c — Concurrent-handshake stress

Open N parallel handshake attempts against the server; verify:

- Below `mqc-handshake-bytes-total-cap`: all succeed.
- Above the cap: excess attempts hit `HANDSHAKE_BYTES_CAP` cleanly,
  no crashes, no leaked file descriptors.
- After the cap-hitting clients disconnect: the in-flight counter
  returns to 0 and new handshakes succeed.

Drives issue-12's atomic counter under realistic concurrency
pressure.

### 4d — Cosigner-rotation drill

End-to-end test of issue-8's cosigner-fingerprint cache invalidation
against the real `admin_recosign` workflow:

1. Run a baseline handshake; verify `~/.TPM/peers/<index>/cosigner-fingerprint.txt`
   exists and matches the current cosigner.
2. Run `admin_recosign` to rotate.
3. Run another handshake against the same peer; verify the cached
   cert is invalidated, re-fetched, and re-verified under the new
   cosigner pubkey.
4. Confirm in `journalctl` the cache-eviction reason was
   "cosigner fingerprint mismatch."

### 4e — Revocation drill (fail-closed under simulated outage)

Tests issue-7's `mandatory` policy against simulated log failures:

1. Block outbound HTTP from the dev client to the log endpoint
   (iptables rule).
2. With cache cold for a peer, attempt handshake; must abort with
   `REVOCATION_QUERY_FAILED`.
3. With cache warm, attempt handshake; must succeed (cache hit
   doesn't need the network).
4. Switch policy to `cache-only`, repeat with cold cache; must
   abort with `REVOCATION_CACHE_MISS`.
5. Restore network; cleanup.

### 4f — Attack-simulation pack

Run the negative-test scenarios from across the plans as a
single pen-test pass:

| From plan | Attack |
|---|---|
| #1 | role-swap, cert-swap, KEM-substitution, empty-ctx replay, version-mismatch, suite-mismatch |
| #1 | mqc-id constant-blob replay (encrypted-mode old-style server signature) |
| #4 | Finished MAC tamper, key-mismatch, AEAD tamper, data-before-Finished, replay |
| #5 | length-tamper, direction-flip, frame-type confusion, sequence-skew, label-removal |
| #8 | HTTP MITM substitute / corrupt, expired cert, cosigner stale-cache |
| #9 | name-mismatch, substituted-server, IP-no-override |
| #11 | duplicate-key, trailing-garbage, comments, leading-zero, int-overflow, unknown-field |
| #12 | wrong KEM length, wrong sig length, cert-rate-limit, handshake-bytes-cap |

Driver harnesses these into one `make test-attack-pack` target.
Every attack must produce the documented `MQC_SECURITY` log line
and the connection must abort.  A pass is "100% of attacks
correctly rejected"; anything less is a Phase-4 fail.

### 4g — Performance baseline

Capture handshake latency before and after Phase 1's rollout
(against a known-good baseline pulled before the cutover):

- Median + p95 + p99 latency for clear-mode handshake.
- Same for encrypted-mode handshake.
- Throughput (handshakes/sec) under saturating load with a single
  client, on the dev box.

Acceptable regression: ≤ 50 % increase in median latency (the
new transcript hash, two ML-DSA calls per handshake under issue-1's
both-sides-sign, plus Finished MAC, plus AAD computation, plus
strict JSON parsing should add a few ms but not double the
wall-clock).  Anything beyond 50 % triggers a profile pass to
identify the regression.

### 4h — 7-day production soak on factsorlie.com

After Phases 0–3 land in production, run for one week with normal
traffic patterns.  Daily review of `journalctl` for:

- Any `MQC_SECURITY` line that indicates a legitimate-client
  failure (i.e., expected-to-pass that didn't).  Any such line
  means a real bug to fix.
- Counter checks: per-IP and per-cert rate-limit triggers should
  fire only on known scanners, not on real clients.
- Crash count: zero.
- Memory drift: stable, no leak suggested by `ps aux`.

### Phase 4 exit criteria

- 4a: aggregate regression suite passes 100 %.
- 4b: 96-cell round-trip matrix passes 100 %.
- 4c: concurrency stress passes; counter returns to baseline after
  load drops.
- 4d, 4e: drills pass.
- 4f: 100 % of attack scenarios rejected with documented
  diagnostics.
- 4g: latency regression within budget.
- 4h: 7-day production soak with zero legitimate-client failures
  and zero crashes.

Only after all 4a–4h pass can Phase 5 begin.

---

## Phase 5 — Spec consolidation (after testing)

Each per-issue plan ships its own targeted spec edits to
`socket-level-wrapper-MQC/draft-page-mqc-protocol-00.md`.  Phase
5 is the **editorial** pass that turns those piecemeal edits
into a coherent revision.

The spec stays at draft-page-mqc-protocol-**00**.  Per the
deployment-model rule (no published -01, single deployment,
nothing requires draft-number coordination with anyone), iterating
in -00 is the right move.  Bumping to -01 would be appropriate
only if the draft were submitted to IETF for tracking; that is
not happening.

### 5a — Coherence pass

Read the cumulative spec end-to-end as if for the first time.
Look for:

- **Cross-reference rot**: a §10.5 sentence that points at "see
  Section 11.2" still resolves to the right content after the
  per-phase edits added/renumbered subsections.
- **Terminology drift**: pick one of "data plane" / "application
  data" / "post-handshake traffic" and use it uniformly.  Same
  for "frame" / "record" / "message" / "blob."  The per-phase
  edits used different terms in different places; consolidate.
- **Style consistency**: capitalization of MUST/SHOULD/MAY per
  RFC 2119, formatting of pseudocode blocks, table styles for
  IANA-style registries.
- **Hash inputs everywhere identical**: the transcript construction
  appears in §6, §7, §8, §10.2, and Appendix A.  Make sure all
  four references describe the exact same byte string, character-
  for-character.

### 5b — Refresh Appendix A worked example

Appendix A today shows a clear-mode handshake with truncated hex.
The old example is wrong after Phase 1 (no `version`/`suite`/`mode`
fields, signature over wrong bytes).  Regenerate from the new
implementation:

1. Run a real clear-mode handshake against factsorlie.com with
   trace-level logging.
2. Capture EK_c, CT_s, both `cert_index` values, both signatures,
   the transcript hash bytes.
3. Truncate to 32 hex chars + `…` per the existing convention.
4. Replace Appendix A with the new dump.
5. Add a parallel encrypted-mode worked example showing the 4-frame
   sequence.
6. Add a third worked example showing the Finished MAC computation
   (the inputs to `HMAC-SHA256(finished_key, transcript_hash_full)`
   and the resulting tag).

### 5c — Add "Changes from prior text" appendix

A short appendix listing every substantive change the cumulative
edits introduced, organized by section:

```
Appendix C.  Changes (informative)

This revision of draft-page-mqc-protocol-00 differs from the
text published initially as follows:

§4   — Suite ID row added.
§5.2 — version, suite, mode fields added; strict parsing rules.
§6   — Handshake signature redefined as transcript-binding.
§7   — Encrypted mode resequenced from 3 to 4 frames.
§8   — HKDF Extract+Expand split; new §8.1 Finished frame.
§9.1 — New §9.1.1 AEAD AAD definition.
§9.2 — IV-XOR-counter nonce; data plane begins at seq=1.
§10  — §10.1 transport-untrust; §10.5 mandatory revocation;
       §10.6 validity window; §10.7 expected identity.
§11  — Operational tunables list; per-(IP, cert) throttling.
§12  — New subsections 12.7, 12.8, 12.9, 12.10.
```

This is the change log a future reader needs to understand "what
moved" without diffing two large versions of the same draft.

### 5d — Render and validate

```
cd socket-level-wrapper-MQC
python3 render-draft.py        # produces draft-page-mqc-protocol-00.txt
```

Check the rendered text:

- Section numbering is contiguous (no §10.5 followed by §10.7).
- Pseudocode blocks render without losing leading whitespace.
- Tables are aligned.
- No markdown leftovers (`**bold**` in the .txt).
- ASCII-only (no smart quotes / em-dashes from editor auto-replace).

### 5e — Index update

The reference list (§14) gains [FIPS204] / [RFC8446] / [RFC8174]
references that the per-phase edits implicitly cite.  Walk the
final body, collect every `[…]`, ensure each is in §14.1 or §14.2.

### Phase 5 exit criteria

- Coherent end-to-end read with no cross-reference rot.
- Appendix A reflects the implemented protocol byte-for-byte.
- Appendix C "Changes" is complete.
- `render-draft.py` produces a clean .txt that matches the .md.
- Spec is the source of truth for any future implementer trying
  to write a second MQC client from scratch.

