## Phase 6 — User-facing documentation (after spec)

The spec (Phase 5) is for implementers writing a second MQC.
Phase 6 is for **operators** running postWolf and **callers** of
the existing MQC tools, plus the **Claude Code instructions** that
future agentic sessions inherit.

### 6a — CLI tool help text + man pages

Every MQC client tool gains new flags during Phases 1–3:

| Tool | New flags | From |
|---|---|---|
| `show-tpm` | `--expected-name`, `--no-name-check` | issue 9 |
| `bootstrap_ca` | `--expected-name`, `--no-name-check` | issue 9 |
| `bootstrap_leaf` | `--expected-name`, `--no-name-check` | issue 9 |
| `admin_recosign` | `--expected-name`, `--no-name-check` | issue 9 |
| `mqc` | `--expected-name`, `--no-name-check` | issue 9 |

Update each tool's `--help` output and the corresponding
`mtc-keymaster/tools/c/*.1` man page (or create the man page if
none exists yet) to include:

- The new flags with one-line descriptions.
- A "Common usage" example showing the most common dial pattern.
- A "Security notes" section pointing operators at
  `--no-name-check` only as a deliberate opt-out, not a casual
  workaround for cert misconfiguration.

Each tool's `--help` should also surface the operator knobs that
affect its behavior, e.g.:

```
$ show-tpm --help
...
SECURITY KNOBS (set in /etc/postWolf/config under [global]):
  mqc-revocation-policy   {mandatory, cache-only, disabled}
                          default: mandatory.  See README-mqc.md.
  mqc-revoked-cache-ttl-sec   default: 86400.
  ...
```

### 6b — Top-level README updates

| File | What to add |
|---|---|
| `mtc-keymaster/README.md` | Section on MQC v0 security properties post-Phase-1: transcript binding, mandatory revocation, name binding.  One-paragraph summary per property + pointer to the relevant per-issue plan for detail. |
| `mtc-keymaster/server2/c/README-using-mtc-server.md` | New "MQC tunables" section listing every `[global]` key the server reads from `/etc/postWolf/config` (the 14 keys across issues 6a, 7, 12).  Per-key: meaning, default, when to tune, sanity ceiling. |
| `socket-level-wrapper-MQC/README.md` (create if absent) | "Calling MQC from C" section: the `mqc_ctx_t` API, when to call `mqc_ctx_set_expected_name`, what `MQC_SECURITY` log lines mean and how to interpret them. |
| `mtc-keymaster/README-bugsandtodo.md` | Close issues #6 and #10 (subsumed by #1).  Add a pointer from the existing TODO #9b (cosigner TOFU) to the master plan's "Out of scope" note. |
| `socket-level-wrapper-MQC/README-mqc-issues.md` | Annotate each of the 12 reviewer issues with the per-issue plan that addressed it.  E.g., "Issue #1 — addressed by `mqc-issue-1.plan`, landed in commit ABC under Phase 1." |

### 6c — Operator runbook

Create `mtc-keymaster/server2/c/README-mqc-operator-runbook.md`
(or extend `README-using-mtc-server.md`) with:

- **Routine procedures**:
  - Tightening or loosening rate limits.
  - Switching `mqc-revocation-policy` between modes (with the
    "when each is appropriate" framing from the issue-7 plan).
  - Lowering `mqc-revoked-cache-ttl-sec` for compliance drills.
- **Incident playbooks**:
  - Log endpoint unreachable: switch to `cache-only`, then
    `disabled` only as last resort.  How to revert.
  - Cosigner pubkey rotation: run `admin_recosign`, expect cache
    invalidations across all peers, monitor for failures.
  - Suspected DoS: how to read the per-IP and per-cert
    rate-limit log lines, where Redis stores the buckets, how
    to manually unban.
- **Log-line decoder**: every `MQC_SECURITY` log line the
  Phase-1–3 changes can emit, with the cause and remediation.
  This becomes the operator's grep target when something goes
  wrong.

### 6d — `/etc/postWolf/config` example

`mtc-keymaster/read-config/config.server` is currently the
operator's reference for what keys exist.  After Phases 0–3 it
needs to grow to include every new tunable, all commented out
with the default value shown:

```
[global]

# --- URLs (existing) ---
url-bootstrap     https://factsorlie.com:8445
url-server        https://factsorlie.com:8446
url-local         https://factsorlie.com:8444

# --- MQC handshake budgets (issue 6a) ---
# mqc-handshake-stall-sec       3
# mqc-handshake-total-sec       5

# --- MQC frame-size ceilings (issue 6a) ---
#   Spec §11.3: MAY lower, MUST NOT raise.
# mqc-max-handshake-bytes       131072
# mqc-max-msg-bytes             1048576

# --- MQC rate limits (issues 6a, 12) ---
# mqc-rl-connect-per-min        100
# mqc-rl-connect-per-hour       1000
# mqc-rl-fail-per-min           10
# mqc-rl-fail-per-hour          100
# mqc-rl-cert-per-min           10
# mqc-rl-cert-per-hour          100

# --- MQC caches + skew (issues 6a, 12) ---
# mqc-revoked-cache-ttl-sec     86400
# mqc-sig-freshness-sec         300
# mqc-cert-cache-max-entries    1024
# mqc-handshake-bytes-total-cap 16777216

# --- MQC revocation policy (issue 7) ---
#   mandatory   = query log on cache miss; abort on query failure.
#   cache-only  = use cache; abort on cache miss.
#   disabled    = skip revocation (UNSAFE; emergency use only).
# mqc-revocation-policy        mandatory
```

Each key commented out + default shown so operators can see what
exists without changing behavior.  Uncommenting any line is the
explicit opt-in to a non-default value.

