Phase 3 doesn't strictly require Phase 1 or Phase 2, but
shipping it before Phase 1 would harden a protocol whose
identity binding is still broken — defense in depth on a
broken core is rarely worth the disruption.  Recommended order
is the linear flow shown.

Phases 4–6 are sequential by construction: testing finds bugs
that need code or spec fixes; spec consolidation reads the as-
shipped behavior, not the as-planned behavior; user docs reference
the consolidated spec.  Don't start Phase 5 with Phase 4 still
finding issues; don't start Phase 6 until the spec stops moving.

## Suggested commit boundaries

| # | Branch | Plans / phase | Rough delta |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `phase-20` | Phase 0: 6a | ~250 LoC |
| 2 | `phase-20` | Phase 1: 1+2+3+4+5 (bundled) | ~1500 LoC |
| 3 | `phase-20` | Phase 2a: 7 | ~250 LoC |
| 4 | `phase-20` | Phase 2b: 8 | ~400 LoC |
| 5 | `phase-20` | Phase 2c: 9 | ~300 LoC |
| 6 | `phase-20` | Phase 3a: 11 | ~300 LoC |
| 7 | `phase-20` | Phase 3b: 12 | ~350 LoC |
| 8 | `phase-20` | Phase 4: test harness + suite-of-suites | ~600 LoC of test code |
| 9 | `phase-20` | Phase 5: spec consolidation | ~150 LoC of `.md` |
| 10 | `phase-20` | Phase 6: user-facing docs | ~800 LoC of `.md` + `.1` |

All on `phase-20` per the existing branch convention.  Per the
"Commit granularity" memory entry, the user's default is to
bundle, but Phase 1 is the only place where the bundling is
load-bearing (everything else is meaningful as an independent
incremental hardening).  Operator may choose to bundle Phase 2
or Phase 3 commits if they prefer a single restart per phase.

Phases 4–6 are documentation/test commits, not behavior changes;
they're still on `phase-20` for trail consistency.

## Verification milestones (across phases)

Each phase has its per-plan verification list; the milestones at
the master level:

1. **Post-Phase 0**: server binary respects `[global]` knobs in
   `/etc/postWolf/config` (or runs at compiled defaults if
   absent).
2. **Post-Phase 1**: handshake transcript is fully bound; AEAD
   frames carry AAD; Finished verifies; data plane works under
   the new wire format.  No legitimate-client failures in 24-hour
   soak.
3. **Post-Phase 2**: revocation is mandatory (visible in
   `journalctl` as one revocation query per peer per cache TTL);
   cert validity window checked; server-name binding enforced.
4. **Post-Phase 3**: malformed JSON rejected pre-crypto; per-cert
   rate limit triggers under index-rotation; handshake-bytes cap
   triggers under concurrency stress.
5. **Post-Phase 4**: every per-issue test passes in the aggregate
   harness; the 96-cell client/mode/policy/fixture matrix is
   green; attack-pack rejects 100 % of documented attacks; 7-day
   production soak shows zero legitimate-client failures.
6. **Post-Phase 5**: spec reads coherently end-to-end; Appendix A
   reflects the implemented protocol; Appendix C "Changes" log
   is complete; `render-draft.py` produces a clean .txt.
7. **Post-Phase 6**: a new operator can deploy postWolf, tune the
   knobs, and respond to incidents using only the docs in the
   repo; `--help` on every CLI tool surfaces the new flags;
   `CLAUDE.md` reflects the new MQC properties.

## Spec-edit accumulation

After all phases the spec
(`socket-level-wrapper-MQC/draft-page-mqc-protocol-00.md`)
gains:

- §4 — Suite ID row.
- §5.2 — `version`/`suite`/`mode` fields, strict-parsing
  paragraph, exact-byte-length rule for hex fields.
- §6 — clear-mode handshake redefined around full transcript.
- §7 — encrypted-mode resequenced from 3 to 4 frames.
- §8 — full HKDF-Extract+Expand derivation; new §8.1 Finished
  Frame.
- §9.1 — new §9.1.1 AEAD AAD definition.
- §9.2 — IV-XOR-counter nonce; data plane starts at `seq=1`
  (consolidated rule).
- §10.1 — "untrusted transport" rewrite.
- §10.2 — handshake signature redefined over transcript.
- §10.5 — revocation MUST not MAY; fail-closed default.
- §10.6 (new) — cert validity window.
- §10.7 (new) — expected-identity check.
- §11 — `mqc-revocation-policy` and 6a/12 tunables documented
  as informative.
- §11.2 — per-(IP, cert) throttling rule.
- §12 — new subsections 12.7 (transcript binding), 12.8
  (frame-header AAD), 12.9 (identity vs authority), 12.10
  (JSON parsing hardening).

The cumulative spec change is substantial but stays inside
draft-00.  No -01 needed unless a future protocol break
requires one.

## Out of scope (across all phases)

The 13 issue plans plus this master plan address the entire
content of [`README-mqc-issues.md`](./README-mqc-issues.md).
Items deliberately deferred across plans:

- **Cosigner-pubkey TOFU bootstrap** — tracked under
  `mtc-keymaster/README-bugsandtodo.md` TODO #9b.  Addressed by
  the leaf-bootstrap and CA-bootstrap plan branches in
  `/home/ubuntu/.claude/plans/lets-plan-to-implement-snoopy-rivest.md`.
  This master plan assumes the cosigner pubkey is already a
  trusted local file.
- **PSK / session resumption / 0-RTT** — not in MQC v0; the
  Finished-doesn't-extend-the-transcript decision in #4 is the
  thing that would change if these were added.
- **Exporter keys** (TLS RFC 8446 §7.5).
- **Re-keying / KeyUpdate** post-handshake.
- **Connection IDs / migration** — MQC has none.
- **Wildcard subjects, IDNA hostnames** in #9.
- **Client puzzles / proof-of-work** in #12.
- **ML-KEM-768 → larger KEM migration** (e.g., for higher
  category targets).
- **Equivalent runtime-config plumbing for SLC and MQCP** — same
  pattern as 6a, separate plans.
